  "summary": [
    "I am a Computer Science and Engineering graduate with a deep passion for solving problems through code. Over the past decade, I have immersed myself in competitive programming, which has sharpened my ability to think critically and design efficient algorithms.",
    "Alongside this, I have developed a strong interest in artificial intelligence, machine learning, deep learning and computer vision — areas where I have gained hands-on experience through academic research projects and self-driven learning. I enjoy exploring how data and intelligent systems can solve real-world problems, and I am always looking for opportunities to grow, collaborate and build meaningful technology.",
    "Today I work as an Advanced ICT Officer at CNRS-UNHCR, where I teach students and apply technology in a humanitarian context — and I have written a free, university-level curriculum of 273 lectures on AI, Machine Learning and Deep Learning."
  ],

  "researchStatement": "My research interests centre on computer vision and deep learning for real-world safety and human understanding — particularly in the context of developing countries such as Bangladesh. My undergraduate research tackled pedestrian detection with risk-level classification for Bangladeshi road scenes and vision-based vehicle over-speed detection, and my current work studies real-time facial-expression analysis with YOLOv11. I am seeking a PhD position where I can develop robust, efficient and fair vision models that work reliably outside benchmark conditions — for road safety, humanitarian applications and education.",

